 "story": {
  "headline": "Real NISAR InSAR over Hpakant's landslide-prone mine slopes",
  "context": "Mine-waste slopes around Hpakant fail often, most deadly in the monsoon. 21 real NISAR interferograms (12- and 24-day pairs) from 2026-01-01 to 2026-10-16 measure how the ground moved along the radar's line of sight. 1 patch of steep ground (5.1 ha in all) moved faster than 3x the scatter on flat reference ground (2.7 cm/yr). Each is a place to check, not a confirmed landslide.",
  "radar_insight": "InSAR compares the phase of two passes 12 or 24 days apart: a shift of half the 24 cm wavelength means the ground moved 12 cm closer to or further from the satellite. Only motion along that line of sight is measured, not elevation or downslope movement, and ground that changes too much between passes (dug pits, dense wet forest) loses coherence and cannot be read."
 },
